## Key decisions

| Decision | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| One global title | Preserve one game, balance system, entitlement model, and content roadmap |
| Regional variation | Localize commerce mechanics, pricing, payments, presentation, and events |
| Paid random competitive power | Remove globally |
| Loot boxes in Belgium and the Netherlands | Replace with deterministic acquisition of equivalent content |
| Probability disclosure | Apply globally, with additional Korea- and China-specific controls |
| Pay-to-win | Set a common power ceiling and meaningful earnable route; avoid exclusive purchased power |
| Cosmetics | Make them the principal premium-value category |
| Gifting | Enable culturally adaptable fixed-value gifting with fraud, coercion, and minor safeguards |
| Regional pricing | Use transparent purchasing-power and market bands, not individualized willingness-to-pay pricing |
| Personalization | Personalize relevance, not odds or prices based on vulnerability |
| Minors | Global baseline of stricter spending and random-reward protections |
| Live-ops testing | Require legal, ethical, and harm-metric controls |
| Advocacy groups | Engage transparently and use criticism as governance input |
| Governance | Give an independent cross-functional council power to stop high-risk mechanics |
| Success criterion | Sustainable lifetime value, fairness, retention, and trust—not short-term conversion alone |

## Conclusion

The publisher should not attempt to preserve identical loot-box monetization everywhere. Nor should it create regional versions with different power, progression, or content value.

The durable approach is to maintain **one global game and economy**, supported by a **modular, jurisdiction-aware commerce layer**. Deterministic acquisition, cosmetics, passes, subscriptions, and protected gifting should become the primary revenue engines. Any remaining paid randomness should be cosmetic, supplemental, globally disclosed, auditable, and bounded by a clear maximum cost.

Regional teams can then adapt pricing, payment methods, event framing, social features, and merchandising to Japan, the Gulf, Brazil, the United States, and other markets without changing the competitive substance of the title. Strong governance, functional-equivalence testing, global player protections, and transparent engagement with advocacy groups will make those differences defensible—and keep regulatory variation from fragmenting the game itself.
